          "text": "<task-notification>\n<task-id>a074b1b</task-id>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Research Org-mode format\" completed</summary>\n<result>Now I have gathered comprehensive information. Let me compile the full research summary.\n\n---\n\n# Comprehensive Research: Emacs Org-Mode File Format\n\n## 1. Complete Syntax Specification\n\nThe Org-mode syntax is formally documented at `https://orgmode.org/worg/dev/org-syntax.html`. It defines a hierarchical document model with three main categories: **headings/sections**, **elements** (block-level), and **objects** (inline-level). The notation is descriptive rather than a formal EBNF grammar, and the canonical reference remains the Emacs Lisp implementation itself (as stated by maintainers: \"The latest Org-mode implementation *is* the spec\").\n\n### 1.1 Document Structure\n\nAn Org document is a sequence of **sections** and **headings**. Content before the first heading is called the \"zeroth section\" or \"preamble.\"\n\n```\n[in-buffer settings / zeroth section]\n* Heading 1\n  Section content...\n** Sub-heading\n   More content...\n```\n\n### 1.2 Headings (Headlines)\n\nHeadings are the primary structural element. The full syntax is:\n\n```\nSTARS KEYWORD PRIORITY COMMENT TITLE TAGS\n```\n\nWhere:\n- **STARS**: One or more asterisks (`*`) starting at column 0. The count defines the heading level (1-N, though 1-8 is conventional).\n- **KEYWORD** (optional): A TODO keyword such as `TODO`, `DONE`, or any custom keyword defined by `#+TODO:`. Must be followed by a space.\n- **PRIORITY** (optional): A priority cookie in the form `[#A]`, `[#B]`, or `[#C]` (A is highest). Custom priorities can be defined.\n- **COMMENT** (optional): The literal word `COMMENT` marks the subtree as commented (excluded from export).\n- **TITLE**: The heading text (can contain objects/inline markup).\n- **TAGS** (optional): Colon-delimited tags at the end of the line: `:tag1:tag2:tag3:`. Tags are right-aligned (convention, not required). Tags contain alphanumeric characters, `_`, `@`, and `#`.\n\nExample of a fully-loaded heading:\n```\n*** TODO [#A] COMMENT Buy groceries :shopping:urgent:\n```\n\nThe `ARCHIVE` tag has special meaning (marks subtree for archiving).\n\n### 1.3 Sections and Planning\n\nImmediately after a heading, a **section** may contain:\n\n1. **Planning line** (optional, must be first non-blank line after heading):\n   ```\n   SCHEDULED: <2026-02-18 Wed> DEADLINE: <2026-02-25 Wed> CLOSED: [2026-02-17 Tue 14:30]\n   ```\n   Contains one or more of `SCHEDULED:`, `DEADLINE:`, `CLOSED:` followed by timestamps.\n\n2. **Property drawer** (optional, must come immediately after the planning line or heading if no planning):\n   ```\n   :PROPERTIES:\n   :ID:       some-unique-id\n   :CATEGORY: work\n   :LOCATION: Office Building\n   :LOCATION+: Room 405\n   :END:\n   ```\n   - Node properties use `:KEY: value` syntax\n   - The `+` suffix (e.g., `:LOCATION+:`) appends to a property (multi-line values)\n   - Property names are case-insensitive but conventionally uppercase\n\n3. **Body content**: paragraphs, lists, tables, blocks, etc.\n\n### 1.4 Drawers\n\nDrawers are collapsible regions:\n```\n:DRAWERNAME:\ncontent...\n:END:\n```\n\nSpecial drawers:\n- `:PROPERTIES:` -- Node properties (only valid immediately after heading/planning)\n- `:LOGBOOK:` -- Clock entries and state change notes\n\nCustom drawers use any valid name (uppercase letters, digits, hyphens).\n\n### 1.5 Timestamps\n\n| Type | Syntax | Example |\n|------|--------|---------|\n| Active | `<DATE TIME>` | `<2026-02-18 Wed>` |\n| Inactive | `[DATE TIME]` | `[2026-02-18 Wed 14:30]` |\n| Active range | `<DATE>--<DATE>` | `<2026-02-18 Wed>--<2026-02-25 Wed>` |\n| Inactive range | `[DATE]--[DATE]` | `[2026-02-18 Wed]--[2026-02-25 Wed]` |\n| With repeater | `<DATE +1w>` | `<2026-02-18 Wed +1w>` |\n| With delay | `<DATE -2d>` | `<2026-02-18 Wed .+1m -2d>` |\n| Diary sexp | `<%%(diary-float t 1 2)>` | Elisp diary expression |\n\nRepeater types: `+` (cumulative), `++` (catch-up), `.+` (from completion date).\nDelay types: `-` (warning days before deadline).\n\nTime can include a range: `<2026-02-18 Wed 09:00-10:30>`.\n\n### 1.6 Links\n\n| Type | Syntax |\n|------|--------|\n| Bracket link | `[[PROTOCOL:PATH][DESCRIPTION]]` |\n| Bracket link (no desc) | `[[PROTOCOL:PATH]]` |\n| Angle link | `<https://example.com>` |\n| Plain link | `https://example.com` (auto-detected) |\n\nCommon protocols: `file:`, `https:`, `http:`, `id:`, `custom-id:`, `mailto:`, `shell:`, `elisp:`, `doi:`, etc. Custom protocols can be defined via `org-link-parameters`.\n\nSpecial link types:\n- Internal: `[[*Heading]]`, `[[#custom-id]]`, `[[target]]`\n- File with line: `[[file:path::linenum]]`\n- File with search: `[[file:path::search-text]]`\n- File with heading: `[[file:path::*heading]]`\n\n### 1.7 Blocks\n\n**Source blocks**:\n```\n#+BEGIN_SRC language -n -r :var x=5 :results output :exports both :tangle file.py :noweb yes\ncode content here\n#+END_SRC\n```\n\nHeader arguments include: `:var`, `:results`, `:exports`, `:tangle`, `:noweb`, `:session`, `:dir`, `:file`, `:cache`, `:cmdline`, `:eval`, `:hlines`, `:colnames`, `:rownames`, `:shebang`, `:mkdirp`, `:padline`, `:comments`, `:wrap`.\n\nSwitches: `-n` (number lines), `+n` (continue numbering), `-r` (remove reference labels), `-l \"fmt\"` (label format).\n\n**Other block types**:\n\n| Block | Syntax | Purpose |\n|-------|--------|---------|\n| Example | `#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE ... #+END_EXAMPLE` | Literal/verbatim text |\n| Quote | `#+BEGIN_QUOTE ... #+END_QUOTE` | Blockquote |\n| Center | `#+BEGIN_CENTER ... #+END_CENTER` | Centered text |\n| Verse | `#+BEGIN_VERSE ... #+END_VERSE` | Poetry/verse |\n| Comment | `#+BEGIN_COMMENT ... #+END_COMMENT` | Excluded from export |\n| Export | `#+BEGIN_EXPORT html ... #+END_EXPORT` | Backend-specific raw content |\n| Special | `#+BEGIN_NAME ... #+END_NAME` | Custom blocks (any name) |\n\n**Dynamic blocks**:\n```\n#+BEGIN: clocktable :maxlevel 3 :scope subtree\n...generated content...\n#+END:\n```\n\n### 1.8 Lists\n\n**Unordered**: Lines starting with `-`, `+`, or `*` (when not at column 0) followed by a space.\n\n**Ordered**: Lines starting with `1.` or `1)` (number + period or parenthesis) followed by a space. Counter sets: `[@20]`.\n\n**Description**: Unordered items with `TERM :: DESCRIPTION` syntax.\n\n**Checkboxes**: `[ ]` (unchecked), `[X]` (checked), `[-]` (partial).\n\n```\n- [ ] Unchecked item\n- [X] Checked item\n  - [-] Partially complete\n1. First ordered item\n2. [@5] Starts at 5\n- Term :: Description here\n```\n\n**List continuation rules**: Body text of a list item must be indented to the same level as the item's text (after the bullet). Blank lines between items are allowed but affect interpretation (loose vs. tight lists). A list ends when a line is less indented than the item's bullet.\n\n### 1.9 Tables\n\n**Org tables** use pipe syntax:\n```\n| Name  | Age | City     |\n|-------+-----+----------|\n| Alice |  30 | New York |\n| Bob   |  25 | London   |\n#+TBLFM: $3=remote(other,$2)\n```\n\n- `|---+---+---|` is a horizontal rule (separator)\n- Column width cookies: `|<10>|` in header\n- Alignment: `<l>`, `<r>`, `<c>` in header rows\n- Formulas: `#+TBLFM:` line below table with Calc or Elisp formulas\n- Cell references: `$1` (column 1), `@2` (row 2), `@2$3` (row 2, col 3)\n\n**table.el tables** are also supported (different format with `+` corners).\n\n### 1.10 Paragraphs\n\nA paragraph is any sequence of non-blank lines that does not match any other element pattern. Paragraphs are separated by blank lines. A paragraph can contain objects (inline markup).\n\n### 1.11 Keywords (In-Buffer Settings)\n\n```\n#+KEY: VALUE\n```\n\nCommon keywords:\n- `#+TITLE:`, `#+AUTHOR:`, `#+DATE:`, `#+EMAIL:`, `#+LANGUAGE:`\n- `#+OPTIONS:` (export options like `toc:2 num:t`)\n- `#+STARTUP:` (buffer display options like `overview`, `showall`, `indent`)\n- `#+TODO:` (custom TODO sequences: `#+TODO: TODO WAIT | DONE CANCELLED`)\n- `#+SEQ_TODO:`, `#+TYP_TODO:` (alternative TODO definition forms)\n- `#+FILETAGS:` (tags applied to entire file)\n- `#+CATEGORY:` (category for agenda)\n- `#+PROPERTY:` (file-level properties)\n- `#+SETUPFILE:` (include settings from another file)\n- `#+INCLUDE:` (include file content)\n- `#+MACRO:` (define macros)\n- `#+LINK:` (define link abbreviations)\n- `#+PRIORITIES:` (custom priority range)\n- `#+CONSTANTS:` (constants for table formulas)\n\n### 1.12 Affiliated Keywords\n\nThese attach to the element immediately following them (no blank line between):\n```\n#+NAME: my-table\n#+CAPTION: Sales figures for Q1\n#+ATTR_HTML: :width 80%\n#+ATTR_LATEX: :placement [H]\n| ... |\n```\n\nTypes:\n- `#+NAME:` -- unique identifier\n- `#+CAPTION:` (supports `#+CAPTION+:` for multi-line)\n- `#+ATTR_BACKEND:` -- backend-specific attributes\n- `#+RESULTS:` -- results from code evaluation\n- `#+HEADER:` -- additional header arguments for source blocks\n\n### 1.13 Footnotes\n\n**References**:\n- Named: `[fn:label]`\n- Inline: `[fn:label:definition text]`\n- Anonymous inline: `[fn::definition text]`\n\n**Definitions** (at the start of a line, not inside a heading):\n```\n[fn:label] Definition paragraph that can span\nmultiple lines as long as the continuation is indented.\n```\n\n### 1.14 Comments and Fixed-Width\n\n- Line comments: `# This is a comment` (hash + space at column 0)\n- Fixed-width lines: `: This is fixed-width` (colon + space at column 0)\n- Comment keyword: `#+COMMENT` on a line\n\n### 1.15 Horizontal Rules\n\nFive or more consecutive dashes at column 0:\n```\n-----\n```\n\n### 1.16 LaTeX Environments\n\n```\n\\begin{equation}\nE = mc^2\n\\end{equation}\n```\n\nAlso inline: `$x^2$`, `\\(x^2\\)`, `$$x^2$$`, `\\[x^2\\]`.\n\n### 1.17 Inline Objects (Text Markup)\n\n| Object | Syntax | Notes |\n|--------|--------|-------|\n| Bold | `*bold*` | |\n| Italic | `/italic/` | |\n| Underline | `_underline_` | |\n| Strikethrough | `+strikethrough+` | |\n| Code | `~code~` | No further processing |\n| Verbatim | `=verbatim=` | No further processing |\n| Subscript | `text_{sub}` or `text_sub` | |\n| Superscript | `text^{super}` or `text^super` | |\n| Entity | `\\alpha`, `\\rarr`, `\\nbsp` | Named special characters |\n| Line break | `\\\\` at end of line | |\n| Target | `<<target>>` | Internal anchor |\n| Radio target | `<<<radio target>>>` | Auto-linking target |\n| Statistics cookie | `[2/5]` or `[40%]` | In headings |\n| Macro | `{{{macro(arg1,arg2)}}}` | |\n| Export snippet | `@@backend:raw content@@` | |\n| Inline source | `src_lang{code}` or `src_lang[args]{code}` | |\n| Inline babel call | `call_name(args)` or `call_name[header](args)[header]` | |\n\n**Emphasis marker rules** (from `org-emphasis-regexp-components`):\n- PRE character: emphasis marker must be preceded by whitespace, `-`, `(`, `{`, `'`, `\"`, or beginning of line\n- POST character: emphasis marker must be followed by whitespace, `-`, `.`, `,`, `;`, `:`, `!`, `?`, `)`, `}`, `'`, `\"`, `[`, or end of line\n- The marker character cannot appear at the boundary (the character immediately inside the marker cannot be whitespace)\n- Content cannot span more than `org-emphasis-max-newlines` (default: 1) newline characters\n- The opening and closing markers must be the same character\n\n---\n\n## 2. Canonical Formatting vs. Non-Standard Whitespace\n\n### Canonical (Standard) Formatting\n\n- **Tab width**: Org-mode assumes **tab-width of 8** for indentation calculations. When Org major mode loads, it sets `tab-width` to 8.\n- **Heading stars**: Must start at column 0 with no leading whitespace. Exactly one space after the final star before the TODO keyword or title.\n- **Property drawers**: Must appear immediately after the heading or planning line with no blank lines intervening. Properties are indented by convention (usually 0 or aligned with content) but the parser does not require specific indentation for the `:KEY: value` lines themselves.\n- **Body indentation**: Body text under a heading is conventionally indented to align with the heading text (2 spaces for level 1, 3 for level 2, etc.), but this is **not required** by the parser. `org-indent-mode` provides virtual indentation.\n- **Blank lines**: One blank line between paragraphs. Two blank lines conventionally between top-level headings. Blank lines before/after blocks are conventional but only the first blank line after an element is considered part of that element.\n- **Tags**: Conventionally right-aligned at `org-tags-column` (default column 77), but the parser only requires them to be at the end of the heading line in `:tag:` format.\n- **Keywords**: `#+KEYWORD:` is case-insensitive (e.g., `#+title:` works the same as `#+TITLE:`), but uppercase is conventional.\n- **Block delimiters**: `#+BEGIN_SRC` / `#+END_SRC` are case-insensitive. The content between them is not.\n- **Planning keywords**: `SCHEDULED:`, `DEADLINE:`, `CLOSED:` are case-sensitive and must be uppercase.\n\n### Non-Standard / Variations That Still Parse\n\n- Extra whitespace in heading lines (multiple spaces between components)\n- Lowercase keywords (`#+begin_src` is valid)\n- Mixed indentation (tabs and spaces) -- works but may produce unexpected results if tab-width differs from 8\n- Property drawers with extra blank lines inside (non-standard but may be tolerated)\n- Tags not right-aligned (still parsed correctly)\n- No blank line between heading and body text (valid)\n\n### What Breaks Parsing\n\n- Whitespace before heading stars (not recognized as heading)\n- Blank line between heading and property drawer (property drawer not recognized)\n- Blank line between heading and planning line (planning not recognized)\n- Incorrect drawer syntax (missing `:END:`)\n- Mismatched block begin/end names\n\n---\n\n## 3. Existing Rust Crates for Parsing Org-Mode\n\n### 3.1 orgize (by PoiScript)\n\n- **Repository**: `github.com/PoiScript/orgize`\n- **crates.io**: `orgize`\n- **Status**: The most mature and widely-used Rust Org-mode parser\n- **Approach**: Parses Org documents into a Rowan-based (lossless) syntax tree\n- **Features**:\n  - Headings with TODO, priority, tags\n  - Planning lines (SCHEDULED, DEADLINE, CLOSED)\n  - Property drawers and node properties\n  - All emphasis/markup types\n  - Links (bracket, angle, plain)\n  - Timestamps (active, inactive, ranges, repeaters)\n  - Source blocks, example blocks, and other block types\n  - Tables (org-style)\n  - Lists (ordered, unordered, description)\n  - Footnotes\n  - Keywords\n  - LaTeX fragments and environments\n  - Clock entries\n  - Comments, fixed-width, horizontal rules\n- **Architecture**: Uses a lossless CST (Concrete Syntax Tree) via the Rowan library (same approach as rust-analyzer). This means all whitespace and formatting is preserved in the tree.\n- **Suitability**: Good for read-parse-transform-serialize workflows. The lossless tree is ideal for tools that need to modify and re-emit Org files without losing formatting.\n- **Limitations**: Some edge cases in emphasis parsing and nested constructs may differ from the Emacs implementation. The \"implementation is the spec\" problem means any external parser may diverge.\n- **Maintenance**: Actively maintained as of 2024.\n\n### 3.2 org-rust (org-rust-rs)\n\n- **Repository**: `github.com/org-rust-rs`\n- **Status**: Alternative Rust implementation\n- **Approach**: Aims for comprehensive Org-mode parsing\n- **Notes**: Less widely adopted than orgize. Check the repository directly for current feature completeness.\n\n### 3.3 Other Options\n\n- **tree-sitter-org**: A Tree-sitter grammar for Org-mode. Usable from Rust via tree-sitter bindings. Provides incremental parsing suitable for editors. May not cover all Org-mode constructs.\n- **nom-based custom parsers**: Several projects have attempted custom parsers using the `nom` parser combinator library. These tend to be incomplete hobby projects.\n- **pest-based parsers**: Some attempts exist using the `pest` PEG parser generator.\n\n### Comparison\n\n| Feature | orgize | org-rust | tree-sitter-org |\n|---------|--------|----------|-----------------|\n| Maturity | High | Medium | Medium |\n| Lossless tree | Yes (Rowan) | Varies | Yes (incremental) |\n| Heading parsing | Complete | Complete | Complete |\n| Property drawers | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| All block types | Yes | Partial | Partial |\n| Emphasis/markup | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Tables | Yes | Yes | Basic |\n| Timestamps | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Footnotes | Yes | Partial | Partial |\n| Active maintenance | Yes | Check repo | Community |\n\n**Recommendation**: For a project that needs to parse and potentially re-emit Org files (as suggested by your `org2jsonl` project directory), **orgize** is the strongest choice due to its lossless Rowan-based tree, active maintenance, and the most comprehensive coverage of Org syntax among Rust crates.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Complete Element Types in Org-Mode Syntax Specification\n\nThe specification at `orgmode.org/worg/dev/org-syntax.html` defines three categories:\n\n### 4.1 Greater Elements (Containers)\n\nGreater elements can contain other elements:\n\n1. **Headline** -- `* KEYWORD PRIORITY TITLE TAGS`\n2. **Section** -- Content between/after headlines (including zeroth section)\n3. **Plain list** -- Container for list items\n4. **List item** -- Individual item in a list (contains elements)\n5. **Property drawer** -- `:PROPERTIES: ... :END:`\n6. **Drawer** -- `:NAME: ... :END:` (custom drawers)\n7. **Dynamic block** -- `#+BEGIN: name params ... #+END:`\n8. **Footnote definition** -- `[fn:label] content`\n9. **Inlinetask** -- Headlines with level >= `org-inlinetask-min-level` (typically 15 stars, requires `org-inlinetask` library)\n10. **Quote block** -- `#+BEGIN_QUOTE ... #+END_QUOTE`\n11. **Center block** -- `#+BEGIN_CENTER ... #+END_CENTER`\n12. **Verse block** -- `#+BEGIN_VERSE ... #+END_VERSE`\n13. **Special block** -- `#+BEGIN_NAME ... #+END_NAME` (any name not matching above)\n14. **Table** -- Container for table rows\n\n### 4.2 Lesser Elements (Leaf/Non-Container)\n\nLesser elements cannot contain other elements (they contain objects or raw text):\n\n1. **Paragraph** -- Default element; any text not matching another pattern\n2. **Comment** -- `# text` lines\n3. **Comment block** -- `#+BEGIN_COMMENT ... #+END_COMMENT`\n4. **Example block** -- `#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE ... #+END_EXAMPLE`\n5. **Export block** -- `#+BEGIN_EXPORT backend ... #+END_EXPORT`\n6. **Source block** -- `#+BEGIN_SRC lang ... #+END_SRC`\n7. **Fixed-width** -- `: text` lines (colon + space)\n8. **Horizontal rule** -- `-----` (5+ dashes)\n9. **Keyword** -- `#+KEY: VALUE`\n10. **Babel call** -- `#+CALL: name(args)`\n11. **Clock** -- `CLOCK: [timestamp]` or `CLOCK: [ts1]--[ts2] => HH:MM`\n12. **Planning** -- `SCHEDULED: ts DEADLINE: ts CLOSED: ts`\n13. **Diary sexp** -- `%%(diary-function ...)`\n14. **LaTeX environment** -- `\\begin{env} ... \\end{env}`\n15. **Node property** -- `:KEY: value` (inside property drawer)\n16. **Table row** -- `| cell | cell |` or `|---+---|` (rule row)\n\n### 4.3 Objects (Inline Elements)\n\nObjects appear within paragraphs, headings, and other text-containing elements:\n\n1. **Bold** -- `*text*`\n2. **Italic** -- `/text/`\n3. **Underline** -- `_text_`\n4. **Strikethrough** -- `+text+`\n5. **Code** -- `~text~`\n6. **Verbatim** -- `=text=`\n7. **Entity** -- `\\name{}` or `\\name` (e.g., `\\alpha`, `\\rightarrow`)\n8. **LaTeX fragment** -- `$...$`, `$$...$$`, `\\(...\\)`, `\\[...\\]`\n9. **Export snippet** -- `@@backend:content@@`\n10. **Footnote reference** -- `[fn:label]`, `[fn:label:definition]`, `[fn::definition]`\n11. **Inline babel call** -- `call_name(args)` with optional headers\n12. **Inline source block** -- `src_lang{code}` or `src_lang[headers]{code}`\n13. **Line break** -- `\\\\` at end of line (before newline)\n14. **Link** -- `[[path][desc]]`, `[[path]]`, `<protocol:path>`, plain URLs\n15. **Macro** -- `{{{name(args)}}}`\n16. **Radio target** -- `<<<text>>>`\n17. **Target** -- `<<text>>`\n18. **Statistics cookie** -- `[N/M]` or `[N%]`\n19. **Subscript** -- `_{text}` or `_text`\n20. **Superscript** -- `^{text}` or `^text`\n21. **Table cell** -- Content within `|...|` in a table row\n22. **Timestamp** -- Active, inactive, range, with repeater/delay variants\n23. **Plain text** -- Raw text not matching any other object\n\n### 4.4 Affiliated Keywords (Meta-Elements)\n\nNot standalone elements but metadata attached to the following element:\n- `#+NAME:`, `#+CAPTION:`, `#+CAPTION+:`, `#+ATTR_BACKEND:`, `#+HEADER:`, `#+RESULTS:`\n\n---\n\n## 5. Edge Cases and Parsing Challenges\n\n### 5.1 Context-Dependent Parsing\n\nOrg-mode parsing is **inherently context-dependent** and requires at minimum a two-pass approach:\n\n1. **First pass**: Identify document structure (headings, sections, blocks)\n2. **Second pass**: Parse inline objects within text, respecting `#+TODO:`, `#+OPTIONS:`, and other in-buffer settings that change what constitutes valid syntax\n\nFor example, `#+TODO: TODO WAIT | DONE CANCELLED` defines which words are TODO keywords. A heading `* WAIT Fix bug` can only be correctly parsed (WAIT as keyword vs. part of title) after reading the file-level settings.\n\n### 5.2 Emphasis Marker Ambiguity\n\nThis is the single most challenging parsing issue:\n\n- `*bold*` vs. `2*3*4` (multiplication) -- Is the `*` an emphasis marker or literal?\n- Pre/post character rules determine parsing: emphasis markers must be preceded by specific characters (whitespace, `(`, `-`, `'`, `\"`, `{`, beginning-of-line) and followed by specific characters (whitespace, `.`, `,`, `;`, `:`, `!`, `?`, `)`, `}`, `'`, `\"`, `[`, `-`, end-of-line)\n- Content cannot start or end with whitespace\n- Content cannot span more than N newlines (default 1)\n- The `~` character for code markup creates problems with text like \"~300 lines\" -- workaround: use zero-width spaces\n- Nested emphasis is possible but interactions are complex: `*/bold italic/*`\n\n### 5.3 List Continuation and Termination\n\n- List items end when a subsequent line has less indentation than the item's text column\n- Blank lines within lists create \"loose\" lists (items wrapped in paragraphs in export)\n- Two consecutive blank lines terminate a list unconditionally\n- Determining whether something is a list item vs. a paragraph requires checking indentation relative to the current list context\n- `*` as a list marker is ambiguous with headings at column 0 -- only valid as a list marker when indented\n\n### 5.4 Block Matching\n\n- Block delimiters are case-insensitive: `#+begin_src`, `#+BEGIN_SRC`, `#+Begin_Src` are all valid\n- `#+END_SRC` must match the type of the `#+BEGIN_SRC` (but case-insensitively)\n- Content inside source blocks and example blocks is not parsed for Org syntax\n- Content inside other blocks (quote, center, verse, special) IS parsed for elements and objects\n\n### 5.5 Property Drawer Placement\n\n- A property drawer is **only valid** as the very first element after a heading's planning line (or directly after the heading if there is no planning line)\n- If there is a blank line between the heading and the property drawer, it becomes a regular drawer, not a property drawer\n- The zeroth section can also have a property drawer (must be the very first thing in the file, before any content)\n\n### 5.6 Keyword vs. Affiliated Keyword\n\nA `#+NAME: value` line is:\n- An **affiliated keyword** if followed immediately (no blank line) by an element (table, source block, etc.)\n- A standalone **keyword** if followed by a blank line or another keyword type\n\nThis means the interpretation of a line depends on what comes *after* it.\n\n### 5.7 Timestamp Edge Cases\n\n- Timestamps with diary sexps (`<%%(...)>`) require Elisp evaluation for full interpretation\n- Repeater + delay combinations: `<2026-02-18 Wed +1w -2d>` (repeat weekly, warn 2 days before)\n- Time ranges within a single timestamp: `<2026-02-18 Wed 09:00-17:00>`\n- Active vs. inactive has semantic (agenda) implications but identical parsing structure\n\n### 5.8 Table Parsing\n\n- Table rows starting with `|` are data rows; rows starting with `|-` are horizontal rules\n- `#+TBLFM:` lines immediately after a table are part of the table element\n- Tables end at the first non-pipe, non-TBLFM line\n- Column alignment and width cookies (`<r>`, `<10>`) are within the table data itself\n\n### 5.9 Encoding and Special Characters\n\n- Org files are assumed to be UTF-8\n- Entity replacement (`\\alpha` to alpha) happens during export, not parsing\n- Escaping: `\\*` prevents emphasis, `\\[[` prevents link parsing, `\\,` at the beginning of a line inside a source block prevents keyword interpretation\n- Zero-width spaces (Unicode U+200B) can break markup parsing as a workaround\n\n### 5.10 Indentation Sensitivity\n\n- Heading stars must be at column 0 (no leading whitespace)\n- List items must maintain consistent indentation\n- Fixed-width (`: `) and comment (`# `) must be at column 0 or indented within a list\n- Tab-width of 8 is assumed for indentation calculation -- different settings cause portability issues between environments\n- `org-indent-mode` adds virtual indentation (not in the file) for display purposes\n\n### 5.11 Footnote Definition Scope\n\nA footnote definition `[fn:label]` at column 0 captures all subsequent lines as its content until:\n- Another footnote definition\n- A heading\n- Two consecutive blank lines\n- End of file\n\nThis means footnote bodies can contain arbitrary elements (paragraphs, lists, blocks).\n\n### 5.12 The \"Implementation Is the Spec\" Problem\n\nAs discussed extensively in the Org-mode community:\n- The Emacs implementation **is** the canonical parser. Any behavior of the Elisp `org-element.el` parser is \"correct\" by definition.\n- The `org-syntax.html` document is descriptive, not normative -- it documents the implementation but may lag behind or be ambiguous.\n- External parsers (including all Rust crates) inevitably diverge from Emacs behavior in edge cases.\n- Karl Voit's **orgdown** project attempts to formalize subsets of Org syntax at increasing levels of complexity (starting with `#od1` as the simplest baseline), providing a spec that external parsers can reliably target.\n\n---\n\n## Summary and Recommendations\n\nFor your `org2jsonl` project, the key takeaways are:\n\n1. **Org-mode has approximately 16 greater elements, 16 lesser elements, and 23+ object types** -- all of which may appear in real-world files.\n\n2. **orgize** is the recommended Rust crate. Its Rowan-based lossless CST preserves all whitespace and formatting, which is important for faithful conversion. It covers the vast majority of Org syntax.\n\n3. **The hardest parsing challenges** are: emphasis marker disambiguation, context-dependent keyword interpretation (requiring forward/backward lookahead), list continuation/termination rules, and the property drawer placement constraint.\n\n4. **Canonical formatting** is loosely defined -- Org-mode is tolerant of formatting variations, but certain positional rules (property drawers after headings, planning lines after headings, stars at column 0) are strict requirements that affect semantic interpretation.\n\n5. **For JSONL conversion**, you will need to decide how to represent the hierarchical Org structure in a flat line-oriented format -- whether to flatten to one JSON object per heading/section, per element, or per document.</result>\n<usage>total_tokens: 32813\ntool_uses: 33\nduration_ms: 395549</usage>\n</task-notification>\nFull transcript available at: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-johnw-src-org2jsonl/tasks/a074b1b.output",
