          "text": "<task-notification>\n<task-id>a775ab7</task-id>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Research Rust Org-mode crates\" completed</summary>\n<result>I now have sufficient information to compile a comprehensive report. Here is the full evaluation:\n\n---\n\n## Rust Crates for Parsing Org-mode Files: Comprehensive Evaluation\n\n### Executive Summary\n\nThe Rust ecosystem for Org-mode parsing is **sparse and immature** compared to parsers available in other languages (Emacs Lisp, JavaScript, Python). There are essentially **3 credible Rust projects** and a handful of tangential ones. None achieves full conformance with the canonical Emacs `org-element.el` parser, which remains the de facto specification. A key challenge is that Org-mode **has no formal, independent specification** -- the Emacs implementation IS the spec, with only the community-maintained `orgmode.org/worg/org-syntax.html` document providing partial documentation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. `orgize` (PoiScript/orgize)\n\n**The most mature and actively maintained Rust Org-mode parser.**\n\n| Attribute | Details |\n|---|---|\n| Repository | `github.com/PoiScript/orgize` |\n| Crates.io | `crates.io/crates/orgize` |\n| Latest version | ~0.15.0 (as of late 2024) |\n| Total downloads | ~50,000 |\n| License | MIT |\n| Parsing approach | Custom parser producing an AST |\n\n**Supported Org elements:**\n- Headlines (with TODO keywords, priority, tags)\n- Sections and paragraphs\n- Ordered, unordered, and description lists\n- Tables\n- Source blocks (`#+BEGIN_SRC ... #+END_SRC`)\n- Links (regular, angle, plain)\n- Emphasis markup (bold, italic, underline, strikethrough, code, verbatim)\n- Timestamps (active, inactive, diary)\n- Properties and property drawers\n- Planning lines (SCHEDULED, DEADLINE, CLOSED)\n- Keywords (`#+KEY: value`)\n- Comments and comment blocks\n- Example blocks, export blocks, verse blocks\n- Footnotes (inline and reference)\n- Fixed-width areas\n- Horizontal rules\n- LaTeX fragments and environments\n- Entity references (`\\alpha`, etc.)\n- Superscript and subscript\n\n**Round-tripping:** Orgize supports serialization back to Org format. The API includes a handler/visitor pattern where you can implement custom handlers for traversal. There is an `OrgHandler` for writing back to Org syntax, and an `HtmlHandler` for HTML export. The library underwent a significant rewrite around version 0.10 (reportedly adopting a `rowan`-inspired lossless syntax tree approach), which improved fidelity in round-tripping.\n\n**Known limitations:**\n- No support for Babel code execution or tangling (these are runtime features, not syntax)\n- Agenda-related features are not part of the parser (those are application-level)\n- Dynamic features requiring Emacs Lisp evaluation (e.g., file-local `#+TODO` keyword sequences, `#+SETUPFILE` inclusion) cannot be handled\n- Edge cases in table formula parsing, complex list indentation, and deeply nested markup may diverge from the Emacs parser\n- The 0.x version number signals the author considers the API not yet stable\n\n**Assessment:** Orgize is the **clear first choice** for any Rust project needing to parse Org-mode. It covers the vast majority of syntax elements that matter for document processing. Its visitor/handler architecture makes it extensible for custom export formats including JSON.\n\n---\n\n### 2. `org-rs` (org-rs)\n\n**An ambitious project aiming for full compatibility with the Emacs Org parser, but incomplete.**\n\n| Attribute | Details |\n|---|---|\n| Repository | `github.com/org-rs/org-rs` (referenced in a CSDN blog post) |\n| Crates.io | Not confirmed as published |\n| Status | Incomplete / in development |\n| Approach | Reimplementation of Emacs `org-element.el` parsing algorithms in Rust |\n\n**Stated goals (from project description):**\n- Full compatibility with the original Emacs Org-mode parser by following the Elisp parsing algorithms\n- Performance and memory safety through Rust\n- C FFI interface for embedding in other applications\n- Planned applications: VS Code/Vim/Sublime integration, Language Server Protocol implementation, export to Markdown/HTML, TaskWarrior integration\n\n**Current state:**\n- The project description indicates focus is on \"completing the element parser\"\n- No confirmed crates.io publication with download statistics\n- The development status appears stalled or very early-stage\n- No evidence of a usable release\n\n**Round-tripping:** Not confirmed as implemented.\n\n**Assessment:** org-rs has the most ambitious design goals but the **least evidence of being usable**. Its approach of faithfully reimplementing the Elisp algorithms is theoretically the most correct path, but the project appears to have not reached a production-ready state. Treat as a research reference, not a dependency.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `tree-sitter-org` (milisber/tree-sitter-org)\n\n**A Tree-sitter grammar for Org-mode, with Rust bindings available through tree-sitter's standard binding generation.**\n\n| Attribute | Details |\n|---|---|\n| Repository | `github.com/milisber/tree-sitter-org` |\n| Also see | `github.com/zac-garby/tree-sitter-org-mode` (alternative) |\n| Crates.io | Not published as standalone Rust crate |\n| Type | Tree-sitter grammar (JavaScript grammar.js generating C parser) |\n\n**Supported elements:**\n- Headlines and sections\n- Lists and checkboxes\n- Code blocks\n- Links and formatting\n- Properties and metadata\n- Tables\n\n**How Rust integration works:** Tree-sitter grammars generate C parsers which can be used from Rust via the `tree-sitter` crate (currently ~v0.25). You would add the generated parser as a build dependency. The grammar produces a concrete syntax tree (CST), not an AST, meaning it preserves all whitespace and formatting.\n\n**Known issues:**\n- A Zed editor issue (`zed-industries/zed/issues/42657`) reports that tree-sitter-org-mode crashes when invoked through Rust bindings\n- Tree-sitter grammars for Org-mode are not mature or standardized\n- The incremental parsing approach is excellent for editors but may not produce the semantic structure needed for document transformation\n- nvim-orgmode explicitly chose NOT to use tree-sitter-org for its core functionality, implementing its own Lua parser instead\n\n**Round-tripping:** Tree-sitter produces a CST (concrete syntax tree) that preserves the full source text, so round-tripping is inherently supported at the text level. However, semantic manipulation (changing TODO states, rearranging headings) would require building additional tooling on top.\n\n**Assessment:** Best suited for **editor integration** (syntax highlighting, folding, navigation) rather than programmatic document manipulation. The Rust integration path is indirect (through tree-sitter's C-to-Rust binding layer) and has reported stability issues.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Other / Tangential Crates\n\n#### `incodoc`\n| Attribute | Details |\n|---|---|\n| lib.rs | `lib.rs/crates/incodoc` |\n| Status | Work in progress |\n\nA document format crate that accepts Org-mode as one of several input formats (alongside Markdown). Supports a **subset** of Org-mode: emphasis, links, code blocks. Does NOT support tables, drawers, properties, planning lines, timestamps, or other advanced features. Primarily focused on rendering to ANSI terminal or HTML output. Not suitable as a general Org-mode parser.\n\n#### `org-core` (v0.0.6)\nReferenced on lib.rs in config-related listings. Handles Org-mode file operations like fuzzy search, outline extraction, ID-based lookups, and heading content retrieval. Very early version (0.0.6). Appears to be a higher-level utility crate rather than a full syntax parser.\n\n#### Custom / Blog projects\nDavid Briggs (dpbriggs.ca) describes building a custom Org-mode parser in Rust using parsing combinators because existing libraries did not meet his needs. This is not a published crate.\n\n---\n\n### Comparison Against Official Org-mode Syntax\n\nThe canonical reference is `orgmode.org/worg/org-syntax.html` and the `org-element.el` source code. Org-mode defines approximately **33 element types** and **22 object types**:\n\n**Greater Elements:** headline, section, center-block, drawer, dynamic-block, footnote-definition, inlinetask, item, plain-list, property-drawer, quote-block, special-block, table\n\n**Lesser Elements:** babel-call, clock, comment, comment-block, diary-sexp, example-block, export-block, fixed-width, horizontal-rule, keyword, latex-environment, node-property, paragraph, planning, src-block, table-row, verse-block\n\n**Objects:** bold, citation, citation-reference, code, entity, export-snippet, footnote-reference, inline-babel-call, inline-src-block, italic, line-break, latex-fragment, link, macro, radio-target, statistics-cookie, strike-through, subscript, superscript, table-cell, target, timestamp, underline, verbatim\n\n| Feature Category | orgize | org-rs | tree-sitter-org | incodoc |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Headlines + TODO/priority/tags | Yes | Partial | Yes | No |\n| Sections & paragraphs | Yes | Partial | Yes | Basic |\n| Plain lists (all types) | Yes | Partial | Yes | No |\n| Tables | Yes | Unknown | Yes | No |\n| Source blocks | Yes | Unknown | Yes | Basic |\n| Property drawers | Yes | Unknown | Partial | No |\n| Planning (SCHEDULED/DEADLINE) | Yes | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Timestamps | Yes | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Links | Yes | Unknown | Yes | Basic |\n| Emphasis markup (all 6 types) | Yes | Unknown | Yes | Basic |\n| Footnotes | Yes | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| LaTeX fragments | Yes | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Clocking | Partial | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Babel calls | No | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Citations (org-cite) | Unknown | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Macros | Unknown | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Inline src/babel | Unknown | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Dynamic blocks | Unknown | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Export snippets | Unknown | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Radio targets | Unknown | Unknown | Unknown | No |\n| Round-trip fidelity | Good | N/A | Excellent (CST) | N/A |\n| Active maintenance | Yes | Stalled | Low | Low |\n| Published on crates.io | Yes | No | No | Yes |\n\n---\n\n### JSONL-related Org-mode Tools\n\nThere are **no existing tools** that directly convert Org-mode files to JSONL (JSON Lines) format. The closest related efforts are:\n\n1. **ox-ipynb** (Emacs Lisp): Exports Org to Jupyter notebook format (which is JSON, but not JSONL)\n2. **orgajs** (JavaScript/npm): Parses Org-mode into an AST in JavaScript; could theoretically produce JSON output, but not JSONL specifically\n3. **org-db-v3** (Emacs Lisp, by John Kitchin): Uses `org-element-parse-buffer` to extract structured data from Org files for database indexing -- conceptually similar to what an org-to-JSONL tool would need\n\nBuilding an Org-to-JSONL converter would require combining a parser (orgize being the best Rust option) with a serialization layer that emits one JSON object per line for each Org entry/heading.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendations for an org2jsonl Project\n\n1. **Use `orgize`** as the parsing foundation. It is the only Rust crate with sufficient maturity, active maintenance, and element coverage.\n\n2. **Expect to handle edge cases manually.** No external parser perfectly replicates Emacs. The key areas of divergence are:\n   - File-local `#+TODO` keyword customization\n   - `#+SETUPFILE` transclusion\n   - Complex table formulas\n   - Babel execution results\n   - Context-dependent parsing (e.g., list indentation rules)\n\n3. **For round-tripping needs**, orgize's rowan-inspired approach preserves source fidelity reasonably well, but do not expect byte-for-byte identity after parse-then-serialize cycles.\n\n4. **The Orgdown (OD1) subset specification** effort by Karl Voit is worth monitoring. If you constrain your tool to the Orgdown subset, you gain a more tractable problem with clearer boundaries.\n\n5. **No off-the-shelf JSONL tool exists** -- this would be a novel contribution to the ecosystem.</result>\n<usage>total_tokens: 40704\ntool_uses: 57\nduration_ms: 434060</usage>\n</task-notification>\nFull transcript available at: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-johnw-src-org2jsonl/tasks/a775ab7.output",
